Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to **obtain a reactive queue** called `"taskQueue"` from a Redisson client while telling Redisson to use a **custom JSON codec** (`JsonJacksonCodec`).  
I’ll walk through the key parts, then give you a ready‑to‑copy code snippet that you can drop into a Spring Boot project (or run as a plain Java application).

---

## 1️⃣ What you need

| Item | Why |
|------|-----|
| **Redisson dependency** | Provides the reactive API (`redisson-reactive`) |
| **Jackson dependency** | Needed by `JsonJacksonCodec` |
| **A `RedissonClient` bean** | Gives you access to all Redisson services |
| **Your domain class** (`Task`, e.g.) | The type you’ll put into the queue |

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
    <version>3.23.2</version> <!-- check for latest -->
</dependency>

<!-- If you’re not using the starter -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson</artifactId>
    <version>3.23.2</version>
</dependency>
```

---

## 2️⃣ Create the Redisson configuration

If you’re on Spring Boot, just expose a `RedissonClient` bean.  
If you’re writing a plain Java app, you’ll build the `Config` manually.

```java
// Spring Boot – RedissonConfig.java
@Configuration
public class RedissonConfig {

    @Bean(destroyMethod = "shutdown")   // ensures graceful shutdown
    public RedissonClient redisson() throws IOException {
        Config cfg = new Config();

        // Example: single server at localhost:6379
        cfg.useSingleServer()
           .setAddress("redis://127.0.0.1:6379");

        // Optional: set password, timeout, pool size, etc.
        // cfg.setPassword("mypassword");

        return Redisson.create(cfg);
    }
}
```

---

## 3️⃣ Obtain the *reactive* queue

```java
import org.redisson.api.RReactiveQueue;
import org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec;

// Inside a @Component / @Service
@Component
public class TaskRepository {

    private final RReactiveQueue<Task> taskQueue;

    public TaskRepository(RedissonClient redisson) {
        // ① Name of the Redis list
        // ② Custom codec – here we use the built‑in JsonJacksonCodec
        this.taskQueue = redisson.getReactiveQueue(
                "taskQueue",
                new JsonJacksonCodec()
        );
    }

    /* ---------- CRUD helpers ---------- */

    /** Push a task onto the tail of the queue */
    public Mono<Void> enqueue(Task task) {
        return taskQueue.offer(task).then();   // returns Mono<Void>
    }

    /** Take (and remove) a task from the head of the queue */
    public Mono<Task> dequeue() {
        return taskQueue.poll();               // returns Mono<Task>
    }

    /** Peek without removing */
    public Mono<Task> peek() {
        return taskQueue.peek();               // returns Mono<Task>
    }

    /** Get current length of the queue */
    public Mono<Long> size() {
        return taskQueue.size();               // returns Mono<Long>
    }
}
```

### How it works under the hood

* `RReactiveQueue<T>` is part of Redisson’s **RxJava 2/3** integration (`redisson-reactive` module).
* When you call `getReactiveQueue(name, codec)` you’re telling Redisson:
  * “Give me a reactive wrapper around the Redis list called `<name>`.”
  * “Serialize/deserialize elements using `<codec>`.”  
  * Here we pass `new JsonJacksonCodec()` which uses Jackson’s `ObjectMapper` to convert between Java objects and JSON bytes stored in Redis.

---

## 4️⃣ Consuming tasks reactively

Here’s a simple consumer that keeps pulling tasks until the queue is empty, then waits a bit before trying again.

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class TaskConsumer {

    private final TaskRepository repo;

    public void startConsuming() {
        repo.dequeue()
            .repeatWhenEmpty(Flux.interval(Duration.ofSeconds(5))) // poll every 5s when empty
            .subscribe(
                task -> process(task),
                err -> log.error("Error consuming task", err),
                () -> log.info("Finished consuming")
            );
    }

    private void process(Task t) {
        // Your business logic here
        System.out.println("Processing: " + t);
    }
}
```

---

## 5️⃣ Using the *tool* you mentioned

If you want to see how the underlying Redisson method looks like (the one exposed by the tool), it’s essentially:

```java
// Pseudo‑signature from the tool description
RedissonRx_getQueue({
    name: "taskQueue",
    codec: "JsonJacksonCodec"
});
```

In real Java code, that translates to the line above where we call `redisson.getReactiveQueue(...)`.

---

## 6️⃣ Quick checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| ✅ | Add `redisson-spring-boot-starter` (or `redisson` + `redisson-reactive`) |
| ✅ | Configure `RedissonClient` bean |
| ✅ | Use `redisson.getReactiveQueue("taskQueue", new JsonJacksonCodec())` |
| ✅ | Work with the returned `RReactiveQueue<Task>` via its reactive methods (`offer`, `poll`, `peek`, `size`, …) |
| ✅ | Handle backpressure & errors appropriately in your subscriber chain |

That’s it! Drop the snippets into your project, replace `Task` with your own POJO, and you’ll have a fully reactive queue backed by Redis, serialized as JSON. Happy coding!

